---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Grid, Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Replenish Primer & Guide - Premodern MTG"
    image="/src/assets/banners/replenish.webp"
    description="The definitive Replenish primer and strategy guide for the Premodern MTG format. Covers maindeck and sideboard analysis, matchups, mulligan strategy, and advanced mechanics."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Replenish Primer & Guide"
        description="<strong>DON'T PANIC.</strong> The mostly harmless, yet wholly definitive Replenish primer and guide for Premodern MTG."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/replenish.webp"
        imageAlt="Replenish"
        imageCaption="Replenish by Jim Nelson"
        isCard={false}
    />
    <Card>
        <p>
            Welcome, weary traveler. This compendium is an expanded, interactive
            adaptation of the
            <a
                href="https://squeeshouseofpizza.com/en-brl/pages/replenish-primer-brian-siu"
                target="_blank"
                class="guide-link">excellent primer</a
            >
            originally penned by <strong>Brian Siu (Dr Siuss)</strong>. It has
            been redesigned, upgraded, and enriched with insights from the
            <a href="/conclusion" class="guide-link">Premodern Replenish Discord community</a>
            to serve as your ultimate resource—so grab your towel, ensure your tray 
            table is securely stowed, and let's cheat some enchantments into play.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="What is Replenish?" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            <strong>Replenish</strong> is a classic combo-control deck in Premodern
            Magic: The Gathering that uses its namesake sorcery to cheat big,
            expensive enchantments onto the battlefield straight from the graveyard.
            The plan is simple: fill and filter your graveyard with
            draw engines like <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
            > and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search"
                >Frantic Search</a
            >, and then resolve a single four-mana <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
            > to take over the board.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1.25rem;">
            The Core Combo: The Wave Lock
        </h3>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
            The deck's primary win condition revolves around a powerful stack
            interaction between two key enchantments:
        </p>
        <ul class="pl-md mb-0">
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong
                    ><a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                        >Opalescence</a
                    >:</strong
                > Transforms all of your non-Aura enchantments into creatures with
                power and toughness equal to their respective mana values.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong
                    ><a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                    >:</strong
                > Normally used to temporarily exile up to five target creatures.
                However, because <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                > animates Wave into a creature,
                <em>it can target itself</em>.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            By stacking four exile triggers targeting opposing creatures and
            then using the final fading counter to exile the <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
            > itself, Wave leaves play <em>before</em> its initial exile triggers
            resolve. Because its return-to-play clause triggers and resolves while
            nothing has been exiled yet, your opponent's creatures are exiled permanently
            once the remaining stack clears, and your Wave returns to the battlefield
            refreshed with five new counters. Incorporating <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
            > into this loop hard-counters every spell your opponent tries to cast
            for the rest of the game.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Why Replenish?" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            If you enjoy explosive combo finishes backed by the tools to grind
            through long, technical games against control and aggro, Replenish
            is an excellent match.
        </p>
        <ul class="pl-md mb-0">
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Strategic Patience:</strong> Rewards careful sequencing,
                tight resource management, and sharp late-game play.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Tactical Depth:</strong> Lots of tough decisions—you're
                setting up your graveyard ahead of time and working the stack
                to get through messy board states.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Versatility:</strong> Shifts between racing to the lock,
                playing defensive permission control, and beating down with animated
                enchantments as the game changes.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-sm">
                <strong>Favorable Positioning:</strong> Naturally beats up on
                midrange piles, draw-go control, and board-based engines
                like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Argothian Enchantress">Enchantress</a
                >.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Strengths and Weaknesses" titleTag="h2">
        <Grid
            column={{ md: 2 }}
            gap="md"
            style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 2rem;"
        >
            <Card>
                <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-success);">
                    Strengths
                </h3>
                <ul class="pl-md">
                    <li class="mb-sm">
                        <strong>Explosive Wins:</strong> A single resolved <a
                            href="#"
                            class="card-hover"
                            data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                        >
                        assembling the Opal-Wave-Decree lock usually wins the game
                        on the spot.
                    </li>
                    <li class="mb-sm">
                        <strong>Role Flexibility:</strong> Can pivot between pure
                        combo, defensive control, or aggressive beatdown depending
                        on the matchup, with disruption like <a
                            href="#"
                            class="card-hover"
                            data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                        > to lock down the early game.
                    </li>
                    <li class="mb-sm">
                        <strong>Strong Matchups:</strong> Favorably positioned against
                        classic Premodern staples, including Enchantress, midrange
                        piles, creature beatdowns, and slow control variants.
                    </li>
                    <li class="mb-sm">
                        <strong>Post-Ban Positioning:</strong> With <a
                            href="#"
                            class="card-hover"
                            data-card-name="Parallax Tide">Parallax Tide</a
                        >
                        out of the format, opposing blue decks no longer threaten
                        instant land sweepers, freeing up sideboard and maindeck
                        slots for your own plan.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </Card>

            <Card>
                <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-danger);">
                    Weaknesses
                </h3>
                <ul class="pl-md">
                    <li class="mb-sm">
                        <strong>Graveyard Dependence:</strong> Inherently vulnerable
                        to hate pieces like <a
                            href="#"
                            class="card-hover"
                            data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                        >, <a
                            href="#"
                            class="card-hover"
                            data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace"
                            >Phyrexian Furnace</a
                        >, and
                        <a
                            href="#"
                            class="card-hover"
                            data-card-name="Planar Void">Planar Void</a
                        > (though post-sideboard creature transitions mitigate this).
                    </li>
                    <li class="mb-sm">
                        <strong>Multi-Axis Aggression:</strong> Vulnerable to decks
                        that come at you from two angles at once (like a fast
                        clock backed by mana denial or heavy countermagic).
                    </li>
                    <li class="mb-sm">
                        <strong>Faster Combos:</strong> Can struggle against ultra-fast
                        linear engines like <a
                            href="#"
                            class="card-hover"
                            data-card-name="Phyrexian Devourer"
                            >Devourer Combo</a
                        > that can combo off before your early disruption comes online.
                    </li>
                    <li class="mb-sm">
                        <strong>Mana Hungry:</strong> Primary engine spells sit at
                        three and four mana, meaning you often have to play defensively
                        until your card selection engines stabilize.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </Card>
        </Grid>
    </Card>

    <Card>
        <p
            style="margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center; color: var(--w-color-primary-30);"
        >
            Ready to explore? Continue to the History of the Deck to trace how
            Replenish evolved across two decades of competitive play.
        </p>
    </Card>
</Layout>
